## Context

`/gaze`'s phase 2-4 "Agent C — PR review" is specified to fan out its own
five-perspective panel (correctness, consistency, scope, red-team,
doc-propagation) as a recursive Agent-spawn: `skills/gaze/SKILL.md`'s "Fork
execution contract" states "The contract applies recursively... a reviewer
Agent (e.g. Agent C) that itself spins a panel of perspective agents...
inherits this same rule" — it must launch its perspective agents in one
message and poll their artifacts, exactly like `/gaze`'s own top-level
fan-out.

During round 2 of `/gaze` on `search-works-for-zotero` PR #553 (2026-09-13),
the agent running Agent C's role had no `Agent` tool in its own toolset. It
did not error or flag this — it silently ran all five perspectives itself,
sequentially, in one pass. The gate's `dissent: none` then reflected one
agent unable to disagree with itself, not five independent reviewers
agreeing. The internal battery still caught a real liveness bug and four
wrong numbers in that run, so the defect did not produce a wrong verdict
here, but it silently weakened the decorrelation guarantee the panel exists
to provide, in exactly the "reported as run while its seats silently never
worked" shape `SKILL.md`'s own "Panel integrity" section (ticket 0393) warns
against for the *external* reviewer seats — this is the same failure shape
one layer in, on the *internal* panel.

## Relevant files

- `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` — "Agent C — PR review" (phase 2-4) and "Fork
  execution contract" § "The contract applies recursively".
- Whatever launches Agent C (the gaze orchestrator's own Agent call, or
  `/review-pr`'s own definition if invoked as a fresh agent) — the tool
  grant for that launch needs to include `Agent` itself so the spawned agent
  can recurse.

## Actions

1. Confirm the tool grant given to the Agent C launch site (in `/gaze`'s own
   orchestration and/or `/review-pr`'s own agent definition) includes the
   `Agent` tool.
2. If missing, add it.
3. Regardless, add an explicit self-check to `/review-pr`'s embedded
   procedure: if it needs to fan out to perspective agents and finds it has
   no `Agent` tool, say so in its returned structured block (a
   `PANEL-INTEGRITY`-style flag) rather than silently running every
   perspective itself and reporting `dissent: none` as if independent
   agreement occurred.

## Test

- Red first: spawn Agent C's role without the `Agent` tool (a toolset that
  excludes it) and confirm it currently proceeds sequentially with no
  error/flag in its returned block.
- Green: with the fix, either the tool is present and fan-out genuinely
  occurs (confirmed by distinct sub-agent artifacts under
  `.panel/<pr>/`), or its absence is loudly flagged in the structured output
  instead of silently absorbed.

## Verification

- [ ] Agent C, launched exactly as `/gaze` launches it in production, has the
      `Agent` tool and can spawn its own perspective sub-agents.
- [ ] A run where fan-out is genuinely unavailable produces a visible
      `PANEL-INTEGRITY`-style flag in the returned block and the posted PR
      comment, not a silent `dissent: none`.

## Invariants

- `dissent: none` in a gate verdict must mean "N independent perspectives
  agreed," never "one agent ran N passes."
- No change to the actual review content or judgment of the five
  perspectives — only to how they are parallelized and how a failure to
  parallelize is surfaced.

## Exit criteria

Agent C's five-perspective panel genuinely fans out to independent
sub-agents in a production `/gaze` run, or, when it cannot, the verdict
comment visibly flags reduced decorrelation instead of reporting
`dissent: none` as if full fan-out occurred.
